Atomically dispersed precious metals are presently receiving considerable attention in catalysis due to their unique chemical activities and large per-atom conversion yields. Conventional systems, i.e. metal particles deposited on oxides by impregnation methods, typically yield broad size distributions, thus reducing the per-atom yield. Although the presence of single atom active sites has been demonstrated in a number of cases,[1] the typically rather amorphous nature of the metal/oxide interface with a high heterogeneity of metal clusters and particles have made the unambiguous identification of reaction mechanisms and the accurate validation of theoretical results very difficult. In this context, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) offer a number of interesting features. First, many of these crystalline coordination polymers obtained by connecting metal or metal/oxo clusters via organic linkers feature coordinately unsaturated metal sites (CUS). In the MOF case, the metal atoms are prevented from sintering by strong ionic bonds to the molecular struts forming the framework. Furthermore, these metal single atom sites are located at well-defined positions of a crystalline, porous lattice and are thus homogeneously dispersed within the MOF. Consequently, MOF-based materials hold great promise as single-site catalysts.[2] However, in many cases it is unclear whether the observed catalytic activity is related to metal ions of the perfect framework, or whether the active sites are related to structural defects within the MOF. In fact, defect engineering of MOFs has been used in numerous cases to tune the structural, electronic and chemical properties of MOFs in a controlled fashion.[3]
MOF powders, the most common form of MOF materials, often exhibit large defect densities even before introducing them intentionally, and there is no case known where an unambiguous identification of active sites and the reaction mechanisms together with a fully satisfying theoretical analysis has been reported.
In order to achieve an atomic-level understanding of single active sites and the mechanism governing a prototype reaction, low-temperature CO oxidation, we have carried out an extensive experimental and theoretical study based on defect-free HKUST1 thin films (surface-mounted MOFs, SURMOFs) fabricated using liquid-phase quasi-epitaxy.[4] The oxidation of carbon monoxide was then investigated by combining infrared reflection absorption spectroscopy (IRRAS) and quantum chemical calculations based on density functional theory (DFT) as well as complete active space (CAS) calculations to treat transition states with multireference character.[5]
After synthesis of the HKUST-1 SURMOF, the structure of the porous framework was characterized using $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (XRD, Supporting Information, Figure S1) and the defect density (i.e. the concentration of $\mathsf{C u^{+}}$ species) was determined by IRRAS using CO as a probe molecule. As shown in Figure 1a, there is only one IR band at $2174~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ characteristic for CO bound to the axial positions of pristine $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ paddle-wheel units (Figure 1b).[3c,6] This data as well as the deconvoluted Cu $2{\mathsf{p}}$ XPS results (Supporting Information, Figure S2a) allow setting an upper limit of $2\%$ to the concentration of the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ species.
For the pristine HKUST-1 SURMOF, the activity for CO oxidation was found to be very low. In Figure S3 we show the insitu IRRAS data recorded during exposing the CO-saturated SURMOF to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ at $105~\mathsf{K}$ . The intensity of the ${\mathsf{C O-C u}}^{2+}$ band remained nearly unchanged in oxygen atmosphere. This result is not consistent with conclusions reported in previous work,[7] the reasons will be discussed below.
In order to unravel the role of defects in this system, we introduced $\mathsf{C u^{+}}$ -containing defects in a controlled fashion by using a thermal defect-engineering (DE) strategy.[8] Briefly, subjecting the pristine, virtually perfect HKUST-1 SURMOF to annealing in UHV at elevated temperatures leads to an oxidative decarboxylation, causing a reduction of the $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ paddlewheel units, thus yielding $\mathsf{C u^{+}}/\mathsf{C u^{2+}}$ defect pairs. A thorough XPS analysis yields a $\mathsf{C u^{+}}$ concentration of $30\%$ created by heating the pristine MOFs at $430~\mathsf{K}$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ (Supporting Information, Figure S2b). The IRRAS data, obtained after exposure of these DE-HKUST-1 SURMOFs to CO, clearly reveal a vibrational band at $2118\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Figure 1a). This band, red-shifted by $56\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ relative to CO bound to $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ species within a perfect paddle wheel, is characteristic for CO ligated to $\mathsf{C u^{+}}$ species.[3c,6] Figure 1c shows the DFT-optimized atomic structure of CO species bound to $\mathsf{C u^{+}/C u^{2+}}$ dimer defects. The computed red shift of ${\mathsf{C O-C u^{+}}}$ vs. $\mathsf{C O-C u^{2+}}(50\mathsf{c m^{-1}})$ is in excellent agreement with the experimental results.
Importantly, the loss of the carboxylate group reducing one of the Cu ions to yield $\mathsf{C u^{+}/C u^{2+}}$ creates additional open space.
Based on the DFT calculations, a second CO can bind to the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ ion, see Figure 1d. Accordingly, the band at $2153\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Figure 1a) is assigned to the asymmetric stretching mode of geminal $(\mathsf{C O})_{2}$ dimers at the $\mathsf{C u^{+}}$ CUS sites. As shown in Figure 2a and b, the DE-HKUST-1 SURMOF exhibits pronounced catalytic activity for the CO oxidation reaction. Even at low temperatures of $105\mathsf{K},$ exposure to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ leads to a substantial reduction of the ${\mathsf{C O-C u}}^{2+}$ vibrational band at $2180~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . While the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ product was observed inside HKUST-1 powders,[7] for the very thin $(100\ \mathsf{n m})$ SURMOFs the amount was too small to be detected.
On the basis of these observations we propose that the catalytic activity of HKUST-1 for CO oxidation results from the presence of reduced $\mathsf{C u^{+}/C u^{2+}}$ dimer defects, thus challenging the interpretation put forward in previous work.[7] In order to further corroborate this hypothesis and to unravel the detailed reaction mechanism, a comprehensive set of DFT and ab initio calculations using the Turbomole[9] and ORCA[10] program packages was carried out.
We started with calculations at the M06 level of theory[11] employing an intact paddle wheel unit and found that CO and $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ are not able to adsorb simultaneously on the same $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ metal site, but could exchange with one another with a relatively low barrier (less than $0.2\ {\mathsf{e V}},$ ). We performed an extensive search for a transition state of CO oxidation with molecular $\O_{2}$ (and vice versa) on the pristine paddle-wheel. Based on relaxed scans (see Supporting Information for more details) the smallest barrier is estimated to be higher than $2\mathsf{e V}$ , no barriers with lower energies were found. On the basis of this rather extensive set of calculations we conclude that low-temperature CO oxidation at the intact $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ pairs is not possible.
We thus turned our attention to the reduced $\mathsf{C u^{+}/C u^{2+}}$ dimers. First, the extra electron available at the $\mathsf{C u^{+}}$ ion increases the CO binding energy to almost twice the value for the fully oxidized case, $\Delta\mathsf{G}=0.38$ vs $0.16~\mathsf{e V}$ (at $100~\mathsf{K})$ . Second, as a result of the additional space (see above), now $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ and CO can bind simultaneously on the metal site, see Figure 3. Starting from a $\overline{{\mathsf{O}_{2}}}$ - $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}{-}\mathsf{C u}^{+}{-}\mathsf{C O}$ complex (III), the reaction path for the overall oxidation of CO contains two transition states, (TS-I) where the first $\complement\mathrm{-}\complement$ bond forms and (TS-II) that leads to the formation of the second C−O bond with simultaneous cleavage of the O−O bond. We note that TS-I has significant multi-configurational and multireference character. The calculated value for M06 is over $1.2\mathsf{e V}$ , while CASSCF[12] and NEVPT2[13] that explicitly account for the multi-reference character calculate TS-I to 0.56 eV (see Supporting Information for more details). This value is in fair agreement with the experimental observation of CO oxidation at temperatures as low as $105\mathsf{K}.$ . TS-II on the other hand, can be successfully treated with single-reference methods and has a barrier of $0.43\mathsf{e V}$ (see Supporting Information for more details).
While the activation energies related to this reaction path are fully consistent with the experimental findings, the presence of an uncharged $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ species is somewhat surprising (see Figure 4a and b). In previous reaction schemes for CO oxidation, typically activated oxygen species such as superoxo or peroxo as well as surface lattice oxygen species were considered.[14] It has been reported that the coadsorption of these activated oxygen species with CO can lead to a frequency shift of the CO stretching vibration due to electronic modifications (see e.g. $\mathsf{A u}/\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ or $\mathsf{A u}/Z\mathsf{n O}^{[16]};$ . This was not observed in the present IRRAS data.
A crucial test for the proposed reaction mechanism in Figure 3 is to demonstrate the presence of the activated $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ species adsorbed on $\mathtt{C u}$ CUS. While the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ stretching vibration is IRinactive in the gas phase, the weak binding to metal cations results in a small transition dipole moment, so that an observation by IR should, in principle, be possible.[17] Indeed, after exposing the pristine HKUST-1 SURMOF to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ at $65~\mathsf{K},$ a very weak signal at $1550~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Supporting Information, Figure S4) was clearly observed, slightly red-shifted with respect to the gas phase value $(1556\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ , [18]  similar to the redshift of $\mathsf{\mathsf{S}}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ predicted by DFT calculations (Figure 4a). Note, that also the framework carboxylate-related vibrations at 1394/1656 cm-1 $(\upnu_{\mathsf{s}}(\mathsf{O C O})/\upnu_{\mathsf{a s}}(\mathsf{O C O}))$ showed small red-shifts to $1386/1650~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Figure 4c, Supporting Information, Figure S4) as a result of the dioxygen adsorption, in line with the DFT calculations (redshift of $^3$ and $5~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , respectively, see Supporting Information for a detailed discussion).
Similar results were observed after $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ adsorption on the DEHKUST-1 SURMOF at $55\mathsf{K}$ (Figure 4c). The IRRAS data shown in Figure 4d allowed identifying two $\O_{2}$ species at 1546 and 1550 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . The spectra recorded after slight annealing revealed that the binding energies of both $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ species are extremely low: the pristine ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -related $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ band at $1550\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ disappears at about $60\mathsf{K}$ while the signal at $1546~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ disappears only after heating to $67~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure 4e). On the basis of DFT calculations, the latter one is assigned to activated, uncharged dioxygen species adsorbed to $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ CUS of the reduced $\mathsf{C u^{+}/C u^{2+}}$ dimers (Figure 4b). Again, the computed frequency $6\ c m^{-1}$ red-shift in comparison to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ on pristine paddle-wheel) and binding energy $(0.1~\mathsf{e V})$ are in good agreement with the experimental results that show the binding only occurs at low temperatures.
After providing strong support for the reaction pathway described in Figure 3 by demonstrating the presence of the uncharged $\O_{2}$ as an important intermediate, we come to the experimental observation that upon exposure to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , for the DEHKUST-1 only the ${\mathsf{C O-C u}}^{2+}$ band decreases in intensity, while the intensity of the band assigned to the ${\mathsf{C O-C u^{+}}}$ , which should be consumed during the reaction, stays virtually constant (Figure 2b).
Considering that the binding energy is larger (by $0.2\mathsf{e V}$ , according to our DFT calculations) in the latter case, this observation can be explained by a quick replenishing of the consumed ${\mathsf{C O-C u^{+}}}$ species by direct transfer from adjacent $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ sites, where CO is more weakly bound. Indeed, a careful analysis of the coveragedependent IRRAS data (Supporting Information, Figure S5) revealed that the activation energy to transfer CO from ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{C u^{+}}$ sites must be lower than $0.20~\mathsf{e V}$ , assuming a diffusion prefactor of $2.5\times10^{-8}\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}/\mathsf{s}$ .[19] Similarly, our DFT calculations result in a binding energy of CO to the ideal pristine paddle-wheel of about $0.4~\mathsf{e V}$ that, taking entropy corrections at $100~\mathsf{K}$ into account, leads to a desorption barrier of $0.16\mathsf{e V}$ .
In summary, on the basis of the experimental observations and the theoretical results, we can provide a fully consistent reaction scheme for low-temperature $\mathtt{\mathtt{C O}}$ oxidation occurring in defectengineered MOFs of type HKUST-I, with the reaction proceeding along the potential path depicted in Figure 3. We find that creating defects in HKUST-1 not only yields higher binding energy adsorption sites for CO, but also additional space to allow for the simultaneous binding of CO and dioxygen on $\mathsf{C u^{+}/C u^{2+}}$ single dimers in a synergistic fashion. The unexpected finding of an uncharged dioxygen intermediate is corroborated by highsensitivity IRRAS data. These results demonstrate that a detailed analysis of reactions in structurally well-defined metal-organic frameworks with their interplay of electronic and steric effects is possible, thus opening the path for a systematic tuning of MOF catalytic properties. Particularly interesting in this context will be mixed-metal systems, for which high catalytic activity has been observed.[20]
